---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import nudgee from "../data/nudgee.json";
import burials from "../data/burials.json";
import arneytown from "../data/arneytown.json";
const { burials: documented, corrections, quarterRow } = nudgee;
const other = burials.filter((b) => !b.place.includes("Nudgee"));
---
<Base title="Nudgee" eyebrow="Nudgee Catholic Cemetery, Brisbane"
      dek="Nineteen of this family are buried here with a plot number and an exact date — and four of those plots are consecutive.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Nudgee Cemetery, beside Holy Cross church at Banyo, has been the Catholic burying
    ground of northern Brisbane since the 1860s. It is run by the Archdiocese, not the city council,
    which is why it does not appear in the Brisbane City Council grave search and why the family tree
    recorded the cemetery but never a plot.</p>
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">The Archdiocese keeps its own register, and it was read on
    <strong>10 September 2026</strong>. It supplies a plot, a register number, a date of birth, a date
    of death and a date of interment for <strong>{documented.length}</strong> people in this
    archive — and it corrects the family tree in <strong>{corrections.length}</strong> places.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The finding</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Four plots in a row</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.9rem 0 1.4rem">Michael Rocco Mazza's four grandparents were born in
    three towns — <a href={u("/piedimonte-etneo")}>Piedimonte Etneo</a> on Etna,
    <a href={u("/briatico")}>Briatico</a> on the Tyrrhenian coast, and
    <a href={u("/scilla")}>Scilla</a> on the strait — about six hundred kilometres apart, in families
    that had never met. They arrived in Australia between 1949 and 1953 on four different sailings.
    They are buried in <strong>four consecutive plots</strong>.</p>

    <div class="rec">
      {quarterRow.map((o) => (<>
        <div class="k">{o.plot}</div>
        <div class="v">
          <a href={u("/people/" + o.slug)} style="color:inherit"><strong>{o.name}</strong></a>
          {o.registerName.toLowerCase() !== o.name.toLowerCase() &&
            <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"> — entered in the register as {o.registerName}</span>}
        </div>
        <div class="w">{o.birth} – {o.death}</div>
      </>))}
    </div>

    <p class="note" style="margin-top:24px">Two married couples, side by side, in the order they
    died: <P n="Rocco Arena" /> in 1999, <P n="Sebastiano Mazza" /> in 2002,
    <P n="Domenica Prostamo" /> in 2010, <P n="Angela Polistena" /> in 2018. Nineteen years between
    the first burial and the last, and the plots were held.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Where the register beats the tree</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">{corrections.length} corrections</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.4rem">A burial register is a contemporary administrative
    record made by the people who did the burying. A family tree is a recollection typed in later.
    Where they disagree, this archive believes the register — and shows you both, so you can disagree
    with it.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Field</th><th>The tree said</th><th>The register says</th><th class="m">Plot</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{corrections.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={u("/people/" + c.slug)}>{c.name}</a></td>
            <td>{c.field === "birth" ? "Born" : "Died"}</td>
            <td style="color:var(--ink-3);text-decoration:line-through">{c.was}</td>
            <td><strong>{c.now}</strong></td>
            <td class="m">{c.plot}</td>
          </tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>One of these is not a mistake but a format.</strong>
    The tree gives <P n="Venera Cavallaro" />'s death as <em>9 June 2013</em>; the register says
    <em>6 September 2013</em>. 9/6 and 6/9 — a date written day-first and read month-first, or the
    reverse. It is the commonest single error in transcribed genealogy and it is worth checking every
    time a date in this archive looks a month or two out.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The nineteen</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Plot</th><th>In the register as</th><th>In this archive</th><th class="m">Born</th><th class="m">Died</th><th class="m">Interred</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{documented.map((o) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="m">{o.plot}</td>
            <td>{o.registerName}</td>
            <td><a href={u("/people/" + o.slug)}>{o.name}</a></td>
            <td class="m">{o.birth || "—"}</td>
            <td class="m">{o.death || "—"}</td>
            <td class="m">{o.interment || "—"}</td>
          </tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="cap">Brisbane Catholic Cemeteries deceased register (Nudgee), searched 10 September 2026.
    Women appear under their married names — <P n="Domenica Prostamo" /> is entered as Domenica Mazza,
    <P n="Angela Polistena" /> as Angelina Arena — which is why a search on a maiden name returns
    nothing and why the archive matches on the spouse's surname too.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The Falco lie here too</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.8rem">This archive keeps meeting the same ground from a
    different direction: <strong>five Falco of Arienzo</strong> are buried at Nudgee, and
    <a href="https://daviddef.github.io/TheFalco/">that archive</a> found them independently, in the
    same register, using the same endpoint. Two families with nothing whatever in common in Italy —
    one from Campania, three from Calabria and Sicily — inside the same fence in Queensland. That is
    not a family connection. It is what a migration looks like from the outside.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Buried elsewhere</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Place</th><th class="m">Died</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{other.map((b) => (
          <tr><td><a href={u("/people/" + b.slug)}>{b.who}</a></td>
              <td>{b.place}</td><td class="m">{b.died || "—"}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="lab" style="margin-top:34px">Answered — 10 September 2026</div>
    <h3 style="font-size:22px;margin-top:.4rem">Arneytown, New Jersey: one soldier, one grave</h3>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">The tree records <strong>three</strong> Polistena at
    the Brigadier General William C. Doyle Veterans Memorial Cemetery. The
    <a href="https://gravelocator.cem.va.gov/" rel="noopener">VA's Nationwide Gravesite Locator</a>
    records <strong>two people, in one plot</strong>:</p>
    <div class="rec">
      {arneytown.map((a) => (<>
        <div class="k">{a.plot.replace("Section ", "").replace("Site ", "")}</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>{a.name}</strong>{a.rank !== "—" ? <> — <strong>{a.rank}</strong>, {a.war}</> : null}</div>
        <div class="w">{a.born} – {a.died}</div>
      </>))}
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>Joseph Polistena served as a Private First Class
    in the United States Army in the Second World War</strong> — that is the service that earned the
    plot. Santa is entered as <em>wife of</em>, in the same site.</p>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px">It also settles two things this archive had open.
    The tree's <strong>"Joseph A Polistena"</strong> and <strong>"Joseph Polistena"</strong> are
    <strong>one man</strong> — which is exactly what the build's middle-initial merge rule concluded
    from the shared death date, before the VA confirmed it. And Santa's death, given in the tree as
    <strong>9 August 2008</strong>, is <strong>8 September 2008</strong>: the American
    <em>09/08/2008</em> read day-first. <strong>That is the second date-format error found in this
    archive today</strong>, after <P n="Venera Cavallaro" />'s.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
